-- A policy is a readable contract about what a script may do.
-- Checked against the parse tree, before anything runs.

forbid running "rm" with "-rf"      -- delete named paths, or use a scratch folder the job owns
forbid running "dd"                 -- nothing here should be writing raw devices
forbid running "chmod" with "777"   -- grant the group that needs it, not everyone
forbid running "sudo"               -- the deploy role already has the permissions it needs

forbid writing to "/etc/*"          -- machine configuration is managed elsewhere
forbid writing to "/usr/*"          -- installed software comes from packages
forbid deleting "/*"                -- a path that broad is never what was meant

warn running "curl"                 -- fine, but say in review what it talks to
warn writing to "/tmp/*"            -- fine, but /tmp is world-readable

require timeout on "curl"           -- an unbounded fetch wedges the whole pipeline
require every command to be checked -- a failure nobody reads is a failure nobody fixes
